<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 3.5 Parametrization of Plane Curves.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  Parametrization.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is
  prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: the pictures that introduce the idea (a general trajectory, the
  unit circle with its parameter marked, the velocity of a projectile, and
  the paths of the two particles) are reproduced in full, as in the lecture
  notes, and every video is kept.  The plots that answer an exercise (the
  parabola from the table, the segment, the ellipse, the shifted circle,
  and the collision picture) become empty grids to draw on, and the table
  of points has its x and y columns left blank.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-geom-parametrization" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Parametrization of Plane Curves</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Describe the motion of a point in the plane with a pair of parametric
equations <m>x = f(t)</m> and <m>y = g(t)</m>, and understand the
parameter <m>t</m> as recording <em>when</em> the point visits each place
on the curve.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Sketch a parametric curve from a table of values, and indicate the
direction of increasing <m>t</m> in which the curve is traced.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations for common curves, such as lines, circles, and
ellipses, and eliminate the parameter to recover a Cartesian equation
when possible.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Model the motion of a projectile with parametric equations, and derive
the equation of its parabolic trajectory.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Distinguish the <term>intersection points</term> of two parametric curves
from their <term>collision points</term>, where two moving objects reach
the same place at the same time.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-parametric-equations">
<title>Parametric Equations</title>

<p>
Imagine that you would like to describe the motion of an object whose
motion is confined to a plane. If you know how its Cartesian
coordinates, <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, change as time passes, you will be
able to draw a curve that represents the trajectory of its motion, as
shown in <xref ref="fig-skel-parametric-motion-video"/>. Mathematically, we
will need two equations to demonstrate how the <m>x</m>- and
<m>y</m>-coordinates evolve in time <m>t</m>, which are known as the
<term>parametric equations</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-parametric-equations">
<title>Parametric Equations</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are given as functions of a third variable
<m>t</m>, called a <term>parameter</term>, by
<md xml:id="eq-skel-parametric-equations" number="yes">
x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t),
</md>
then the points <m>(x,y) = (f(t),\,g(t))</m> trace out a curve in the
plane, called a <term>parametric curve</term>. The equations
<m>x=f(t)</m> and <m>y=g(t)</m> are the parametric equations of the
curve.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-parametric-motion-video">
<caption>
A point moves in the plane. As time <m>t</m> passes, its coordinates
<m>x = f(t)</m> and <m>y = g(t)</m> change, and the point traces out
the trajectory of the motion.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-parametric-motion"
       source="videos/parametric_motion_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-parametric-motion">
<caption>
The trajectory of a moving point whose coordinates are given by the
parametric equations <m>x = f(t)</m>, <m>y = g(t)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-parametric-motion">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>f(t)=(0.62*t*cos(t), 0.62*t*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-7,-4.5,6,7)">
<grid-axes at="pm-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="pm-curve" function="f" domain="(0.4, 9.6)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pm-start" p="f(0.4)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pm-end" p="f(9.6)" fill="red"/>
<label at="pm-eq-label" anchor="(-6.7,6.2)" alignment="e" color="blue">
<m>(x,y)=(f(t),\,g(t))</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing a spiral curve, the trajectory of a moving point whose coordinates are given by the parametric equations x equals f of t and y equals g of t. The curve starts near the origin and spirals outward.">
  <annotation ref="pm-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-curve" text="The trajectory of the motion, a spiral drawn in blue that starts near the origin and winds outward."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-start" text="The starting position of the moving point, near the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-end" text="The final position of the moving point, at the outer end of the spiral."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-eq-label" text="The label x comma y equals f of t comma g of t, indicating that each point of the curve is given by the parametric equations."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
As an example, consider <m>x = t</m> and <m>y = t^2</m>. Fill in
<xref ref="table-skel-parabola-points"/>, plot the seven points on the grid
below, and join them. (Then eliminate the parameter to name the curve, and
mark on your sketch the direction in which it is traced as <m>t</m>
increases. Notice that the left half and the right half are traced in
different directions relative to the origin.)
</p>

<table xml:id="table-skel-parabola-points">
<title>Points on the curve <m>x=t</m>, <m>y=t^2</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
</row>
<row><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>

<sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-4,-1) grid (4,10);
\draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,10.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative four to four horizontally and negative one to ten vertically, for plotting the seven points from the table and joining them.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (7,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {Eliminating $t$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.2) {Name of the curve, and its direction:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for eliminating the parameter and one for naming the curve and the direction in which it is traced.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-parabola-param-video">
<caption>
The parametric curve <m>x = t</m>, <m>y = t^2</m>: the points from the
table appear first, and then the moving point traces out the parabola
<m>y = x^2</m> in the direction of increasing <m>t</m>. Use it to check your
sketch.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-parabola-param"
       source="videos/parabola_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
As our second example, consider the equation of the circle centered at
<m>(0,0)</m> with unit radius,
<md xml:id="eq-skel-unit-circle" number="yes">
x^2 + y^2 = 1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Here, we would like to parametrize <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in terms of a
single parameter <m>t</m>, as in
<xref ref="eq-skel-parametric-equations"/>, in such a way that by varying the
value of <m>t</m> we can reconstruct the circle
<xref ref="eq-skel-unit-circle"/>. Find such a pair. (One trigonometric
identity does the whole job; match it against
<xref ref="eq-skel-unit-circle"/> term by term. State the interval of
<m>t</m> that traces the circle exactly once, and check your answer by
substituting it back in.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {The identity, and the parametrization:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Interval of $t$, and the check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the trigonometric identity and the resulting parametrization and one for the interval of the parameter and the verification.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-unit-circle-param">
<title>The Unit Circle</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-unit-circle-param" number="yes">
x = \cos(t), \qquad y = \sin(t), \qquad t \in [0, 2\pi).
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-unit-circle-param-video">
<caption>
Parametrizing the unit circle: as <m>t</m> increases from <m>0</m> to
<m>2\pi</m>, the point <m>(\cos(t), \sin(t))</m> travels
counterclockwise around the circle, passing through the marked points
at <m>t = 0</m>, <m>\pi/2</m>, <m>\pi</m>, and <m>3\pi/2</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-unit-circle-param"
       source="videos/unit_circle_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-unit-circle-param">
<caption>
Parametrizing the unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> by
<m>x=\cos(t)</m>, <m>y=\sin(t)</m>, <m>t \in [0,2\pi)</m>. A generic
point of the circle corresponds to the parameter value <m>t_0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-unit-circle-param">
<diagram dimensions="(376,240)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t)=(cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,3.2,1.5)">
<grid-axes at="uc-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="uc-curve" function="c" domain="(0, 2*pi)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<line at="uc-radius" endpoints="((0,0), c(pi/5))" stroke="green"/>
<point at="uc-t0-point" p="c(pi/5)" fill="green"/>
<point at="uc-mark-0" p="(1,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-half-pi" p="(0,1)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-pi" p="(-1,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-3half-pi" p="(0,-1)" fill="red"/>
<label at="uc-label-0" anchor="(1,0)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=0</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-half-pi" anchor="(0,1)" alignment="nw" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-pi" anchor="(-1,0)" alignment="sw" color="red">
<m>t=\pi</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-3half-pi" anchor="(0,-1)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-t0" anchor="c(pi/5)" alignment="ne" color="green">
<m>(x,y)=(\cos(t_0),\,\sin(t_0))</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="uc-angle-t0" points="((1,0),(0,0),c(pi/5))" radius="20" clear-background="no">
<m>t_0</m>
</angle-marker>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The unit circle centered at the origin, parametrized by x equals cosine of t and y equals sine of t. Four points at t equals 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2 are marked in red, and a generic green point corresponds to the parameter value t naught.">
  <annotation ref="uc-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-curve" text="The unit circle, drawn in blue, traced counterclockwise as t increases from 0 to 2 pi."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-radius" text="A green radius from the origin to the generic point of the circle at parameter value t naught."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-t0-point" text="A generic point of the circle, with coordinates cosine of t naught comma sine of t naught."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-0" text="The point 1 comma 0, where t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-half-pi" text="The point 0 comma 1, where t equals pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-pi" text="The point negative 1 comma 0, where t equals pi."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-3half-pi" text="The point 0 comma negative 1, where t equals 3 pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-angle-t0" text="The angle t naught between the positive x axis and the radius to the generic point."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

