</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-projectile-motion">
<title>Projectile Motion</title>

<p>
A type of parametric equation you may have already seen is the equation of
motion for a projectile. Here the parameter is time, and <m>(x(t), y(t))</m>
gives the position of the projectile at time <m>t</m>.
</p>

<p>
Recall two basic facts about motion in one dimension from your introductory
physics course. For motion along a straight line at constant speed <m>v</m>,
the position is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-motion-constant-speed" number="yes">x(t) = v\, t + x_0.</md>
For motion with uniform acceleration <m>a</m>, the position is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-motion-uniform-accel" number="yes">x(t) = \frac{1}{2} a t^2 + v_0 t + x_0.</md>
</p>

<p>
Now consider an object moving under the influence of gravity alone, ignoring
air resistance. Write down its equations of motion. (Ask first which of
<xref ref="eq-skel-motion-constant-speed"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-motion-uniform-accel"/> applies in each direction, and
why. Resolve the initial velocity into horizontal and vertical components
using the launch angle <m>\theta</m>, and take the initial position to be the
origin. Watch the sign of the acceleration.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {Components of the initial velocity:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$x(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {$y(t) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the components of the initial velocity, for the horizontal equation of motion, and for the vertical equation of motion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-projectile">
<title>Equations of Motion for a Projectile</title>
<p>
<md number="yes">
  <mrow xml:id="eq-skel-projectile-x">x(t) \amp = v_0 \cos(\theta)\, t + x_0,</mrow>
  <mrow xml:id="eq-skel-projectile-y">y(t) \amp = - \frac{1}{2} g t^2 + v_0 \sin(\theta)\, t + y_0,</mrow>
</md>
where <m>v_0</m> is the initial speed, <m>\theta</m> is the launch angle, and
<m>g</m> is the acceleration due to gravity.
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Now eliminate the parameter to find the trajectory. (Solve
<xref ref="eq-skel-projectile-x"/> for <m>t</m>, with
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (0,0)</m>, and substitute into
<xref ref="eq-skel-projectile-y"/>. Simplify until <m>y</m> is a polynomial in
<m>x</m>, using <m>\sin\theta/\cos\theta = \tan\theta</m>, and then name the
curve you get.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$t =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {Substituting, $y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {The trajectory is a:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the time solved from the horizontal equation, for the substitution that gives y in terms of x, and for the name of the resulting curve.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Differentiating the equations of motion gives the velocity of the projectile.
Write down its two components, and use them to say what happens at the apex.
(Differentiate <xref ref="eq-skel-projectile-x"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-projectile-y"/> with respect to <m>t</m>. One component is
constant <mdash/> say which physical fact that reflects. Then check your
answers against <xref ref="fig-skel-projectile-velocity"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$v_x =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (7.50,7.7) {$v_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {Why $v_x$ is constant:};
