</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-intersection-collision">
<title>Intersection Points versus Collision Points</title>

<p>
A parametrization carries more information than the curve it traces: it also
records <em>when</em> the moving point visits each place on the curve. Because
of this, there are two different questions we can ask about two parametric
curves
<md>
\mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle f_1(t),\, g_1(t)\bigr\rangle, \qquad
\mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle f_2(t),\, g_2(t)\bigr\rangle,
</md>
thought of as the trajectories of two particles.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-intersection-collision">
<title>Intersection Points and Collision Points</title>
<statement>
<p>
A point <m>P</m> is an <term>intersection point</term> of the two curves if
<m>P</m> lies on both curves; that is, if there are parameter values
<m>t</m> and <m>s</m>, <em>not necessarily equal</em>, with
<md xml:id="eq-skel-intersection-condition" number="yes">
\mathbf r_1(t) = \mathbf{OP} = \mathbf r_2(s).
</md>
</p>
<p>
A point <m>P</m> is a <term>collision point</term> of the two particles if
both particles are at <m>P</m> at the <em>same</em> time; that is, if there
is a single value of <m>t</m> with
<md xml:id="eq-skel-collision-condition" number="yes">
\mathbf r_1(t) = \mathbf r_2(t) = \mathbf{OP}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
An intersection point is a statement about the two <em>paths</em>: they cross,
like two roads on a map. A collision point is a statement about the two
<em>motions</em>: the cars are at the crossroads at the same instant.
</p>

<p>
Say how the two definitions differ when you set up the equations, and how they
are related. (One of the two searches for a <em>pair</em> of parameter values
and the other for a <em>single</em> one; say which, and what that means for
the names you give the parameters. Then decide whether every collision point
is an intersection point, and whether the converse holds.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {To find intersection points, solve:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {To find collision points, solve:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Does one imply the other?};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the equations that give intersection points, for the equations that give collision points, and for the implication between the two.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
In the next two examples the two <em>paths</em> are always the same: the
parabola <m>y = x^2</m> and the line <m>y = x+2</m>, which meet at the two
points <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. Only the <em>schedule</em> of the
second particle will change.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-no-collision">
<title>The paths cross, but the particles never meet</title>
<statement>
<p>
Two particles move in the plane with position vectors
<md>
\mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle t,\; t^2\bigr\rangle, \qquad
\mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle -t,\; 2-t\bigr\rangle, \qquad t \in \mathbb R .
</md>
Find all intersection points of their paths, and all collision points of
the particles.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Eliminate the parameter from each to confirm that the paths really are the
parabola and the line named above. Then set up
<xref ref="eq-skel-collision-condition"/>: the two equations must hold for the
<em>same</em> <m>t</m>, and here one of them pins <m>t</m> down immediately.
Finish by finding, for each of the two crossings, the time at which each
particle passes through it <mdash/> that is what shows how they miss.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {Paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {Collisions:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {Times at which each particle visits $(-1,1)$ and $(2,4)$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the two paths after eliminating the parameter, for the collision equations, and for the times at which each particle reaches each crossing.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-no-collision-video">
<caption>
The particles <m>\mathbf r_1(t) = \langle t,t^2\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle -t,2-t\rangle</m> move simultaneously. Their paths cross at
<m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>, but the clock shows that the two particles
are never at either crossing at the same time.
</caption>
<video source="videos/no_collision_1080p.mp4" width="80%"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-no-collision">
<caption>
The parabola <m>C_1</m> and the line <m>C_2</m> intersect at
<m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. At each of these points the two particles
arrive at different times, so neither point is a collision point.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
The parabola <m>y=x^2</m> and the line <m>y=x+2</m> drawn on the same
axes, crossing at the points <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-no-collision">
<diagram dimensions="(340,340)" margins="5">

  <definition>r1(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
  <definition>r2(t) = (-t, 2 - t)</definition>

  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-2,4,6)">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

    <parametric-curve at="path1" function="r1" domain="(-2.45,2.45)"
                      stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
    <parametric-curve at="path2" function="r2" domain="(-4,4)"
                      stroke="red" thickness="2"/>

    <label at="label1" anchor="(2.6,5.6)" alignment="e" clear-background="no">
      <m>C_1</m>
    </label>
    <label at="label2" anchor="(-3.5,-1.2)" alignment="n" clear-background="no">
      <m>C_2</m>
    </label>

    <group at="pointA">
      <point p="(-1,1)" style="circle" fill="black"/>
      <label anchor="(-1.3,1)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
        <m>(-1,1)</m>
      </label>
    </group>

    <group at="pointB">
      <point p="(2,4)" style="circle" fill="black"/>
      <label anchor="(2,4)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
        <m>(2,4)</m>
      </label>
    </group>
  </coordinates>

  <annotations text="Two parametrized paths that intersect in two points, although the two particles never collide">
    <annotation ref="paths" text="The two paths">
      <annotation ref="path1" sonify="yes"
                  text="The path of the first particle, the parabola y equals x squared"
                  speech="The first particle moves along the parabola y equals x squared, with position r one of t equals t comma t squared"/>
      <annotation ref="path2" sonify="yes"
                  text="The path of the second particle, the line y equals x plus 2"
                  speech="The second particle moves along the line y equals x plus 2, with position r two of t equals negative t comma 2 minus t"/>
    </annotation>
    <annotation ref="intersections" text="The two intersection points of the paths">
      <annotation ref="pointA" text="The intersection point negative 1 comma 1"/>
      <annotation ref="pointB" text="The intersection point 2 comma 4"/>
    </annotation>
  </annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-collision">
<title>The same paths, a different schedule<mdash/>now they collide</title>
<statement>
<p>
Keep the first particle, but re-schedule the second one:
<md>
\mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle t,\; t^2\bigr\rangle, \qquad
\mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle 2t-2,\; 2t\bigr\rangle, \qquad t \in \mathbb R .
</md>
Find the intersection points of the paths and the collision points of the
particles.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Eliminate the parameter again and compare with the previous example: the
paths should be unchanged, so the intersection points are too. The collision
equations, however, now have a solution; find it, and remember to check that
the value of <m>t</m> from the first equation also satisfies the second. Then
say what happens at the <em>other</em> crossing, and mark the collision on the
grid below.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (7,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (7,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,9.7) {Paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {Collisions:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,2.7) {The other crossing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the paths, for the collision computation, and for what happens at the other crossing.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-4,-2) grid (4,6);
\draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative four to four horizontally and negative two to six vertically, for drawing the parabola and the line and marking the collision point.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-collision-video">
<caption>
With <m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle 2t-2,2t\rangle</m> the second particle follows the same
line as before, but on a different schedule. Use it to check which crossing is
the collision, and when it happens.
</caption>
<video source="videos/collision_1080p.mp4" width="80%"/>
</figure>

<insight xml:id="skel-insight-collision-vs-intersection">
<title>Every Collision Is an Intersection, but Not Conversely</title>
<p>
If two particles collide at <m>P</m>, then certainly <m>P</m> lies on both
paths, so every collision point is an intersection point. The converse
fails: the paths may cross at a point that the two particles visit at
different times. Changing the parametrization of a curve does not move the
curve, but it can create or destroy collisions.
</p>
</insight>

<exercise xml:id="skel-checkpoint-collision">
<statement>
<p>
Keep <m>\mathbf r_1(t) = \langle t,t^2\rangle</m>. Find a parametrization
<m>\mathbf r_2</m> of the line <m>y = x+2</m> for which the two particles
collide at <em>both</em> intersection points.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$\mathbf r_2(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {The collision equations, and their solutions:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the new parametrization of the line and one for the collision equations it produces.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Try to arrange that the second particle has the same <m>x</m>-coordinate as
the first one at every time <m>t</m>.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
